<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Penthièvre</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Penthièvre is a historic region of northern Brittany, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, around Lamballe and the bay of Saint-Brieuc. It became a county held by a branch of the ducal house of Brittany in the 11th century, and the claim of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joan-of-penthievre">Joan of Penthièvre</a> to the duchy led to the Breton War of Succession of 1341–1364. It later passed to French noble families and was raised to a duchy in 1569.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/penthievre.jpg" alt="Penthièvre" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: GwenofGwened · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Banni%C3%A8re_Maison_Penthi%C3%A8vre.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The countship of Penthièvre, Penteur in Breton, lay in what is now the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotes-darmor">Côtes-d'Armor</a>, and its lands corresponded closely to the territories of the early Breton kingdom of Domnonée. It first belonged to the House of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rennes">Rennes</a>. Alan III, Duke of Brittany, gave it to his brother Eudes in 1035, and Eudes's descendants formed a cadet branch of the ducal house that held it through the 11th and 12th centuries.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The title passed through frequent dispossessions and restorations. Henri d'Avaugour, heir of the family, lost the countship in 1235, and Pierre Mauclerc, Duke of Brittany and founder of the Breton House of Dreux, gave it as a dowry to his daughter Yolande on her marriage in 1238 to Hugh XI of Lusignan, Count of La Marche. Her brother John I, Duke of Brittany, seized it on her death in 1272.</p><p>In 1337 Joan the Lame brought Penthièvre to her husband, Charles de Châtillon, Count of Blois. After the death of Charles in 1364, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-iv-duke-of-brittany">John IV, Duke of Brittany</a>, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-montfort">John of Montfort</a>, was confirmed as duke, and under the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerande">Guérande</a> of 1365 Joan recognised him while keeping Penthièvre.</p><p>A second Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerande">Guérande</a> in 1381 provided that her senior male heir would succeed if the House of Montfort produced no male heir. Her grandson Olivier lost the county after kidnapping and attempting to blackmail <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-v-duke-of-brittany">John V, Duke of Brittany</a>; his brother John II recovered it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-ii">Francis II</a>, Duke of Brittany, deprived Nicole of Blois of Penthièvre in 1465, and after his death in 1488 her eldest son Jean III de Brosse pressed the family's claim to the duchy against Anne, Duchess of Brittany.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The countship was restored to Sébastien de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a>, heir of the Brosse family through his mother, and raised for him into a duchy in the peerage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. His daughter Marie de Luxembourg married Philippe Emmanuel, Duke of Mercœur, governor of Brittany from 1582, who invoked her hereditary rights to make himself independent in the province from 1589, allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and held out against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iv-of-france">Henry IV of France</a> until 20 March 1598.</p><p>Louis Joseph, duc de Vendôme, inherited Penthièvre in 1669, but a decree of 1687 took it from him and adjudged it to Marie Anne de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a>, Princess de Conti. In 1696 it was sold to Louis-Alexandre de Bourbon, Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>, whose son Louis de Bourbon bore the title Duke of Penthièvre, and the title later passed by inheritance to the House of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Counts and dukes of Penthièvre” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The bishopric of Dol and the later counties of <b>Penthièvre</b> and Léon preserved parts of its territory.</blockquote><a href="/state/domnonee">Domnonée</a></li><li><blockquote>Through marriage the viscounty passed to the dukes of Brittany in 1275, then to the Blois-<b>Penthièvre</b> line and the house of Albret.</blockquote><a href="/state/viscounty-of-limoges">Viscounty of Limoges</a></li></ul></section>
